#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130058} :activity/id #uuid "b14c5a1b-dfd3-54ea-b205-2433540d7060" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050299 :topic/id #uuid "3d717848-5743-5882-aa66-adea2ee38d55" :topic/math-academy-id 1980} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130050 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130388 :tutorial/content "Earlier, we claimed that function $f(x)$ is differentiable at $x_0$ *if the function admits a unique tangent line* at $x_0.$ Let's now justify this claim.\n\nRecall that a function $f(x)$ is differentiable at $x=x_0$ with derivative $f'(x_0)$ if and only if the following limit exists:\n\n$$\nf'(x_0) = \\lim_{x\\to x_0}\\dfrac {f(x) - f(x_0)} {x-x_0}\n$$\n\nWe can rewrite this limit as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {x}_{0}}(\\frac{f(x)-f({x}_{0})}{x-{x}_{0}})-{f}^{'}({x}_{0}) & =0 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {x}_{0}}(\\frac{f(x)-f({x}_{0})}{x-{x}_{0}}-{f}^{'}({x}_{0})) & =0 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {x}_{0}}\\frac{f(x)-f({x}_{0})-(x-{x}_{0}){f}^{'}({x}_{0})}{x-{x}_{0}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for the above limit to hold true, the numerator must approach zero. So, near $x_0,$ we must have\n\n$$\nf(x) - f(x_0) - (x-x_0)f'(x_0) \\approx 0,\n$$\n\nwhich can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nf(x) - f(x_0) \\approx (x-x_0)f'(x_0).\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following equation of the tangent line at $x_0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\ny -y_0 = (x-x_0)\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d x}(x_0)\n$$\n\nFrom here, we can say that a function $f(x)$ is differentiable at $x_0$ *if the function admits a unique tangent line* at $x_0.$\n\nWe'll now use a similar idea to define the differentiability of a multivariable function at a point $P$ in terms of tangent planes." :tutorial/id #uuid "48f4e466-1052-5e98-b9a0-d345e3139cb2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3914 :tutorial/title "Defining the Differentiability of Single Variable Functions in Terms of Tangent Lines"} :step/id #uuid "41594163-d4dc-5179-be5c-4adec6fac7bc" :step/math-academy-id 23115 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130051}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130051 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130389 :tutorial/content "Earlier, we claimed that function $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at $(x_0,y_0)$ *if the function admits a unique tangent plane* at $(x_0,y_0).$ Let's now justify this claim.\n\nAssume that $f(x,y)$ is a differentiable function of two variables. Since $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at $(x_0,y_0),$ then there exists a vector $\\nabla f = \\langle f_1, f_2\\rangle$ such that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (x_0,y_0)}\\dfrac {f(x,y) - f(x_0,y_0) - \\langle x-x_0, y-y_0\\rangle \\cdot \\langle f_1, f_2\\rangle} {||\\langle x-x_0,y-y_0\\rangle||} = 0. \\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nFor the above limit to hold, the numerator must approach zero. So, near $(x_0,y_0),$ we must have\n\n$$\nf(x,y) - f(x_0,y_0) - \\langle x-x_0, y-y_0\\rangle \\cdot \\langle f_1, f_2\\rangle \\approx 0,\n$$\n\nwhich can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nf(x,y) - f(x_0,y_0) \\approx \\langle x-x_0, y-y_0\\rangle \\cdot \\langle f_1, f_2\\rangle.\n$$\n\nExpanding the dot product, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y) \\approx  f(x_0,y_0) + (x-x_0)\\cdot f_1+ (y-y_0)\\cdot f_2.\n$$\n\nWe now need to show that $f_1$ and $f_2$ are the partial derivatives.\n\n- Let $y=y_0$ in the first equation $(\\ast)$ above. This gives\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to x_0}\\dfrac {f(x,y_0) - f(x_0,y_0) - (x-x_0) f_1} {x-x_0} = 0,\n$$\n\nwhich is the definition of the partial derivative with respect to $x.$ So, we have $f_1 = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0).$\n\n- Similarly, letting $x = x_0$ gives $f_2 = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0).$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x,y)$ can be linearized as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)\\approx f(x_0,y_0) + (x-x_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0) + (y-y_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0).\n$$\n\nBy setting $z=f(x,y)$ and $z_0 = f(x_0,y_0),$ we get the equation of the tangent plane:\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = (x-x_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0) + (y-y_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0)\n$$\n\nFrom here, we can say that a function $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at $(x_0,y_0)$ *if the function admits a unique tangent plane* at $(x_0, y_0).$\n\nFinally, note that we have also shown that \n\n$$\n\\nabla f = \\left\\langle \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}, \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThe vector $\\nabla f$ is known as the **gradient vector**." :tutorial/id #uuid "3e6cb319-460f-5a22-888e-e3f886ea9b0b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3915 :tutorial/title "Defining the Differentiability of Multivariable Functions in Terms of Tangent Planes"} :step/id #uuid "c0a78a51-4800-5622-8fa8-92069e9e72f6" :step/math-academy-id 23116} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130052 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129183 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129002 :question/id #uuid "a6d910ae-c6db-51d2-93ba-f6c823c0a8c1" :question/math-academy-id "e-6179"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4de868c0-80b7-5cbb-9dc2-ba04493471fc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048713}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129616} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129619} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265734} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323938} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342095} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342096} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342097} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383117} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383124} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383131} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383138} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385584}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Equation of a Tangent Plane"} :step/id #uuid "6b19e61c-814d-510b-93fb-964a9df12d5a" :step/math-academy-id 23118 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130056}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130053 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129184 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129003 :question/id #uuid "4eb4806c-3d5b-5ed2-87a1-d1ed50c267e5" :question/math-academy-id "e-6182"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cc25edf-9d32-5b47-ae4f-e370d2d0d024" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129630} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129631} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265753} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265754} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323970} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342103} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342106} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385598} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403730}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points at Which Tangent Planes Have Particular Properties"} :step/id #uuid "a126e19a-f8b5-5040-9719-0bc0a4c611e3" :step/math-academy-id 23123 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130050}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130055 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130390 :tutorial/content "Recall that the equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z = f(x,y)$ at $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ can be given as\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = (x-x_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0) + (y-y_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0).\n$$\n\nSlightly rewriting the equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0)} \\cdot (x-x_0) + {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0)} \\cdot (y-y_0) + ({\\color{blue}-1}) \\cdot (z - z_0) = 0,\n$$\n\nwhich immediately gives us a normal vector to the tangent plane:\n\n$$\n\\left\\langle {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0)}, \\:\\: {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0)}, \\:\\: {\\color{blue}-1} \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nA normal vector to the tangent plane to the surface is called a **normal vector to the surface**." :tutorial/id #uuid "c5ebd9d6-c219-583a-8bca-87268b8aa8bc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3919 :tutorial/title "Finding a Normal Vector to a Surface"} :step/id #uuid "828fdb16-4d33-565c-9b6f-4db12aed5567" :step/math-academy-id 23133 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130053}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130056 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130391 :tutorial/content "Until now, we considered only functions of the form \n\n![](images/48/4838bea41cd622ef69be5caaafac89df818d35cf108730686d68d7b7a12265ac.svg)\n\nof two independent variables. But what about the case of functions \n\n![](images/72/72e701a6fcd4514adb9731764fc74a1cb3f0b73f548680db162cd2c8a025e0d6.svg)\n\nwith three variables? Can we define a tangent plane to such a function?\n\nThe answer is yes! Analogously to the case with two variables, a function of three variables ![](images/72/72e701a6fcd4514adb9731764fc74a1cb3f0b73f548680db162cd2c8a025e0d6.svg) is differentiable at ![](images/f4/f46650c560f32308db30c404d86da2d5921654805cca770cd2bc3627e55dbf86.svg) if it admits a **unique tangent hyperplane** at ![](images/3f/3fe19998bf33d149c699a01ca363c2691041b3ecfd52c47a167053b4097dbb57.svg) where ![](images/7e/7e2db5c48c69709e3c1d04d4834af7cc0b2ff0df186cf1713e207c164a8e41e5.svg) The equation of the **tangent hyperplane** is given by\n\n![](images/f4/f433839875810dc7417400a22bad7b8a63c0eb1033954a8e91e7049f3ada361a.svg)\n\nNotice that we use the term *hyperplane*. The graphs of functions of three variables live in four-dimensional space. The equation above describes an object (a so-called hyperplane) in four-dimensional space that can be considered an analog to a *plane* in three-dimensional space." :tutorial/id #uuid "d6f087f4-e5d4-59f8-90f7-a5a9484a0c6f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3920 :tutorial/title "The Equation of a Tangent Hyperplane"} :step/id #uuid "773e70e4-737a-5fa4-92ed-0e1d04815ea8" :step/math-academy-id 23134 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130057}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130057 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129186 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129005 :question/id #uuid "ed1a3960-c2c4-57e7-8fd1-daf5d98bf052" :question/math-academy-id "e-6185"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc56f7a5-65a7-53a9-9eb6-ae7b91a1bdd0" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129618} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129863} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265742} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265743} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265744} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265745} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342099} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342100} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342101} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342102} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385591} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407545}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Equation of a Tangent Hyperplane"} :step/id #uuid "ca4b822d-4434-5d75-8400-5106f71e78a9" :step/math-academy-id 23135 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130055}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130058 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130392 :tutorial/content "Similar to tangent lines for single variable functions, we can think of the **tangent plane** to a surface at a point as the plane that \"touches\" the surface at that point.\n\nThe equation of the tangent plane to a surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $(x_0,y_0,z_0),$ where $z_0=f(x_0,y_0),$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = (x-x_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0) + (y-y_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0).\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the surface \n\n$$\nz = 4-x^2-y^2.\n$$\n\nLet's find the equation of the tangent plane to the surface at the point $(0,\\sqrt{2},2),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/03/03df90a94615238e2126338f64d1f51fcefc379068a8cdc366b33b998cd69f45.png)\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y) = (0,\\sqrt{2})\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(0,\\sqrt{2}) & =-2(0)=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(0,\\sqrt{2}) & =-2(\\sqrt{2})=-2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-2 & =(x-0)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(0,\\sqrt{2})+(y-\\sqrt{2})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(0,\\sqrt{2}) \\\\ z-2 & =0(x-0)-2\\sqrt{2}(y-\\sqrt{2}) \\\\ z-2 & =-2\\sqrt{2}y+4 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2}y+z-6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get that the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane is $2\\sqrt{2}y + z - 6 = 0.$\n\n![](images/d5/d51bdbe8820472334f7e1d942c027b6ad8164eaece58220f3f2e5cdb51544f05.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "bced0695-3ebd-5b62-b7ff-9c8f2500fc2a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3921 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0355e3e0-f89b-5dca-97bc-21791fa4aed2" :step/math-academy-id 23153 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130073}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130073 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130402 :tutorial/content "A surface $z = f(x,y)$ is differentiable at a point $P$ if and only if it admits a *unique* tangent plane at $P.$ Intuitively, this means that, in the neighborhood of $P,$ the surface $z=f(x,y)$ can be approximated as a plane.\n\nNote that this is analogous to the case of functions of one variable. A function $y = f(x)$ is differentiable at a point $P$ if and only if it admits a *unique* tangent *line* at $P.$ This is the same as saying that, in the neighborhood of $P,$ the curve $y=f(x)$ can be approximated as a straight line.  \n\nWe'll explore these ideas in more depth at the end of the lesson. For now, let's get some practice at computing tangent planes." :tutorial/id #uuid "015c4e8e-c1ba-5672-8228-ff0869681d6d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4231 :tutorial/title "A Note on Differentiability"} :step/id #uuid "0296b5b5-c2ce-56c6-a8e4-ad0af71e0dde" :step/math-academy-id 25668 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130052}}] :activity/title "Tangent Planes to Surfaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129334}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046665} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407547} :activity/id #uuid "710af205-0718-5ccf-b346-4f23b266524d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050299 :topic/id #uuid "3d717848-5743-5882-aa66-adea2ee38d55" :topic/math-academy-id 1980} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407546 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342103 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52002c6b-9859-5b7b-a1de-586418b14736" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "$(-1,3,28)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339887} {:answer/id #uuid "551d2228-56ee-5488-91bb-0169794e5cbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "$(1,-3,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339916} {:answer/id #uuid "796a4945-abaa-5bd2-997d-087f2262b1b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "$(-1,3,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340216} {:answer/id #uuid "8a645bd4-5f4d-5164-aa70-44b211e15ecc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "$(1,-3,0)$ and $(-1,3,28)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340370} {:answer/id #uuid "c6b33a5b-7afb-5095-9268-a98f89267aca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "$(1,-3,0)$ and $(3,1,20)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340894}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339916} :answer-field/id #uuid "e58edcff-ee66-5f0c-86cd-1ec279fff31c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341793}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c589653c-432a-5bb8-acb2-94698429fbb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-73857" :question/problem "At which points on the surface $z={x}^{2}-2x+{y}^{2}+6y+10$ is the tangent plane horizontal?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-2x+{y}^{2}+6y+10) \\\\  & =2x-2 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-2x+{y}^{2}+6y+10) \\\\  & =2y+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨2x-2,\\,2y+6,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that the tangent plane is horizontal if and only if the normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0:$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}2x-2=0 \\\\ 2y+6=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=1$ while the second equation gives $y=-3.$ \n\nFinally, substituting $x=1$ and $y=-3$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz={1}^{2}-2(1)+(-3)^{2}+6(-3)+10=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point where the tangent plane is horizontal is $(1,-3,0).$"} :step/id #uuid "0eadd7d5-26a1-573f-b533-83cfbfb498b9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407548}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407547 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323970 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "464dff10-0c59-5dad-80a1-2ee16d60dbfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "(2\\pi ,-\\pi ,2\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321709} {:answer/id #uuid "48223b68-6d40-5d37-8bb5-b27364a9cf80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "(2\\pi ,\\pi ,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321721} {:answer/id #uuid "a226c677-0c51-577f-b0f5-1b3064cc6815" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "(\\pi ,2,-2\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322433} {:answer/id #uuid "adf98dbd-c12a-5550-9749-ddcc015f00fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "(2\\pi ,\\pi ,12\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322518} {:answer/id #uuid "d80a3e3a-b37d-53fa-b4ba-7e5d51839fdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "(-2,6,8\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322842}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322433} :answer-field/id #uuid "5baf0726-2cff-5a6b-904f-2992e085ab37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5c9333a9-b5d6-5ec8-b1f3-0c62768fb8b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-37986" :question/problem "At which points on the surface $z=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-xy$ is the tangent plane perpendicular to the vector  $v\\,=\\,⟨8,2\\pi ,2⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-xy) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-xy) \\\\  & =-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y,\\,-x,\\,-1⟩\n$$\n\nOur tangent plane should be perpendicular to $⟨8,2\\pi ,2⟩.$ So, the normal vector of that plane must be parallel to that vector. In other words, for some constant $c$, we must have\n\n$$\nc\\cdot ⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y,\\,-x,\\,-1⟩=⟨8,2\\pi ,2⟩.\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coordinates, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2c\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-cy=8 \\\\ -xc=2\\pi  \\\\ -c=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $c=-2.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-x(-2)=2\\pi \\,⟹\\,x=\\pi .\n$$\n\nAnd substituting $c=-2$ and $x=\\pi $ into the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n2(-2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -(-2)y=8\\,⟹\\,y=2.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=\\pi $ and $y=2$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-xy \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -(\\pi )(2) \\\\  & =-2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane is perpendicular to the vector  $v$ at $(\\pi ,2,-2\\pi ).$"} :step/id #uuid "2b51d9b4-6f4d-519a-946f-18af30633fc4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407550}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407548 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265754 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4831fde7-328b-5f95-8b4f-bd555404f79f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "(-1,-1,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263630} {:answer/id #uuid "5b9d3b86-7aec-5970-92c9-47d3d544786f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "(1,0,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263795} {:answer/id #uuid "94d3b3cb-73a3-5f94-abc9-28adc6612ba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "(0,1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264280} {:answer/id #uuid "9d20a40f-b237-5738-bfa6-76f889ea8789" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{3},-1,\\frac{7}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264351} {:answer/id #uuid "a54e3749-cf7d-5ffc-ac93-9256ee36c08f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{3},0,\\frac{1}{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264421}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264351} :answer-field/id #uuid "38b8a09a-7de4-5c87-b2a5-460a354244c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "12830aeb-cb43-530e-902c-e52ba9d7524f" :question/math-academy-id "q-129557" :question/problem "At which point on the surface $z=3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ is the tangent plane perpendicular to the vector  $v=⟨4,-2,-1⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=6x \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨6x,\\,2y,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nOur tangent plane should be perpendicular to $⟨4,-2,-1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of that plane must be parallel to that vector. In other words, for some constant $c$, we must have\n\n$$\nc\\cdot ⟨6x,\\,2y,\\,-1⟩=⟨4,-2,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coordinates, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}6cx=4 \\\\ 2cy=-2 \\\\ -c=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $c=1.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n6(1)x=4\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nAnd substituting $c=1$ into the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n2(1)y=-2\\,⟹\\,y=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=\\frac{2}{3}$ and $y=-1$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =3{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}+(-1)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane is perpendicular to the vector $v$ at $(\\frac{2}{3},-1,\\frac{7}{3}).$"} :step/id #uuid "32cd70ef-6665-5a4e-a0ab-b70e5098e66f"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407549 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342099 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62fcc3f3-ddff-52b4-b6ee-b406a0ffec98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\pi x-\\pi y-z+w+\\pi =0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340014} {:answer/id #uuid "93a5ca28-3798-5c54-9d92-aa2d41eb6d90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\pi x-\\pi y+z+w-\\pi =0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340459} {:answer/id #uuid "9f8794dd-7491-5377-b367-530775903444" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\pi x+\\pi y+z-w+3\\pi =0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340576} {:answer/id #uuid "f6251148-2e5e-526a-995c-c61130894b01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\pi x+\\pi y-z+w-\\pi =0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341308} {:answer/id #uuid "fa7d3452-6743-53e2-bd9c-8cfa857fa7bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\pi x+\\pi y+z+w-3\\pi =0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341349}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341349} :answer-field/id #uuid "a31f7eac-9e1f-5aaa-97e4-e132312fc936" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341674}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "de6bd964-56b9-511c-9d67-ed8d06732a08" :question/math-academy-id "q-73851" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent hyperplane to the hypersurface $w=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz)$ at the point $(1,1,\\pi ,0).$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the hypersurface $w=f(x,y,z)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0},{w}_{0})$ on the hypersurface can be written as\n\n$$\nw-{w}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(z-{z}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂z}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz))=yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz) \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz))=xz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz) \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz))=xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y,z)=(1,1,\\pi ):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,1,\\pi ) & =(1)(\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot 1\\cdot \\pi )=-\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,1,\\pi ) & =(1)(\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot 1\\cdot \\pi )=-\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,1,\\pi ) & =(1)(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot 1\\cdot \\pi )=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent hyperplane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w-0 & =(x-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,1,\\pi )+(y-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,1,\\pi )+(z-\\pi )\\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,1,\\pi ) \\\\ w & =-\\pi (x-1)-\\pi (y-1)-(z-\\pi ) \\\\ w & =-\\pi x+\\pi -\\pi y+\\pi -z+\\pi  \\\\ \\pi x+\\pi y+z+w-3\\pi  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "536015a5-6f9f-597a-ae46-2c87559ba2b3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407546}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407550 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407545 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cf0dc93-d9d4-5f21-8930-dd5004fc42a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "2x-2y-2z-w-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407539} {:answer/id #uuid "5110fa49-48ce-56a6-b93d-b9d08b07b745" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "2x+2y+2z-w-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407540} {:answer/id #uuid "728a521d-8a62-5203-8384-b89408abe64b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "2x-2y-2z+w-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407541} {:answer/id #uuid "72b11d02-8ab8-5c20-a244-f44e993b6792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "2x+2y-2z-w+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407542} {:answer/id #uuid "e0e8ca25-16b2-54a8-b96c-ad5665775b39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "2x-2y+2z+w-8=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407543}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407539} :answer-field/id #uuid "75cea473-3250-5a97-b3ad-a51baa102dcf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407544}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8ca7d6fc-55d0-503c-8138-64f03d668ed2" :question/math-academy-id "q-73850" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent hyperplane to the hypersurface $w=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2})$ at the point $(1,-1,1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the hypersurface $w=f(x,y,z)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0},{w}_{0})$ on the hypersurface can be written as\n\n$$\nw-{w}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(z-{z}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂z}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}))=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}))=\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}))=-\\frac{2z}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,-1,1) & =\\frac{2(1)}{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}-(1)^{2}}=2 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,-1,1) & =\\frac{2(-1)}{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}-(1)^{2}}=-2 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,-1,1) & =-\\frac{2(1)}{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}-(1)^{2}}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent hyperplane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w-0 & =(x-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,-1,1)+(y+1)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,-1,1)+(z-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,-1,1) \\\\ w & =2(x-1)-2(y+1)-2(z-1) \\\\ w & =2x-2-2y-2-2z+2 \\\\ -2x+2y+2z+w+2 & =0 \\\\ 2x-2y-2z-w-2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "93f5b13c-94e0-5cb3-a51d-2c9c6f5ed98b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407549}}] :activity/title "Tangent Planes to Surfaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407538}]}
